**Definition 2.3.** Let (S, ·) be a semigroup, let n ∈ ℕ and let A ⊆ S. We say that

- A is a thick set if for any finite subset F ⊂ S, there exists an element x ∈ S such that Fx = {fx : f ∈ F} ⊂ A;
- A is a syndetic set if there exists a finite set F ⊂ S such that S = ⋃_{x∈F} x⁻¹A, where x⁻¹A = {y : xy ∈ A};
- A is piecewise syndetic if there exists a finite set F ⊂ S such that ⋃_{x∈F} x⁻¹A is a thick set. It is well known that A is piecewise syndetic if and only if there exists p ∈ K(βS, ·) such that A ∈ p.
- A is central if it belongs to a minimal idempotent in βS.

It can be proved that a set A is thick iff there exists a left ideal L such that L ⊆ Ā. And a set A is syndetic iff for every left ideal L, L ∩ Ā ≠ ∅. Note that if f : (S, ·) → (T, ·) be any map between discrete semigroups. Then f̃ : βS → βT be the continuous extension of f defined by f̃(p) = f̃(p-lim x) = p-lim f(x), where p-lim f(x) = y if and only if for every neighbourhood U of y, {x : f(x) ∈ U} ∈ p. For details about the theory of ultrafilters, we refer to the book [15].

